WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.669
INTRO:
Usually, average people don’t care about

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:05.800
or even know who the federal reserve chair
is.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.970
But ever since the pandemic hit, and Jerome
turned on the money printer, he’s become

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:12.630
one of the biggest memes on the internet.

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:17.420
From making the money printer go brrr to his
promise that inflation is just “transitory”,

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:21.150
Jerome has truly become a legend within the
retail space.

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:25.970
Savers with a lot of cash despise his policies
as they watch their savings evaporate away.

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:30.890
But, investors have had a blast buying the
dip and going to the moon, well at least till

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:31.930
recently.

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:36.920
In the last few Fed meetings, Jerome has become
a bit more hawkish and he’s made it clear

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.389
that interest rates are going up soon.

00:00:39.389 --> 00:00:44.370
And this has resulted in one of the worst
January’s ever in stock market history.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:47.589
But I mean, I don’t think you can really
blame him when you consider that he’s already

00:00:47.589 --> 00:00:53.929
pumped up the money supply by $6 billion wait
never mind $6 trillion within just the last

00:00:53.929 --> 00:00:54.969
2 years.

00:00:54.969 --> 00:01:01.260
So, Jerome has no doubt made his mark in economic
history, but who even Jerome Powell, and how

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.969
did he become the money printer.

00:01:03.969 --> 00:01:09.790
LAWYER POWELL:
Taking a look back, Jerome Hayden Powell was

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:13.670
born on February 4, 1953, in Washington DC.

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:16.510
He was one of 6 children to Patricia and Jerome
Powell.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:19.840
And yes, his father’s name was also Jerome.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.330
His family wasn’t billionaire rich or anything,
but they did hold pretty prestigious positions

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:25.820
within society.

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:30.670
For example, his father was a private lawyer,
and given that they lived in Washington DC,

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:33.010
he likely served for some pretty notable figures.

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:37.260
Similarly, Jerome’s maternal grandfather
was the dean of the Columbus School of Law

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.450
and a lecturer at Georgetown Law School.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:43.440
Considering Jerome’s background, I don’t
think you’d be surprised to hear that Jerome

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.530
was sent to an elite private school called
Georgetown Preparatory School.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:52.850
Currently, for day students, yearly tuition
comes in at $38,330 and for boarding students,

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:55.909
tuition comes in at $62,090.

00:01:55.909 --> 00:01:59.400
This makes it the 4th most expensive boarding
school in the US.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:03.870
Anyway, moving on in Jerome’s elite journey,
for his bachelor's, Jerome attended Princeton

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:06.840
University and majored in politics.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.780
After graduation, Jerome become a legislative
assistant to a Pennsylvania Senator named

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:12.230
Richard Schweiker.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:15.209
He only did this for a year though before
he went back to school.

00:02:15.209 --> 00:02:19.080
This time, he attended Georgetown University
and pursued a law degree.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:23.639
While at Georgetown, Jerome served as the
editor in chief at the Georgetown Law Journal,

00:02:23.639 --> 00:02:29.090
so it looks like he had an interest in journalism,
but he never explored this interest much further.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:33.569
After graduating in 1979, he hopped around
from one job to another as he tried to find

00:02:33.569 --> 00:02:34.569
his calling.

00:02:34.569 --> 00:02:38.459
First, Jerome moved to New York City and started
clerking for a judge in the US court of appeals.

00:02:38.459 --> 00:02:44.290
He did this for 2 years before he switched
over to a law firm called Davis Polk &amp; Wardwell.

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:47.910
He worked here for another 2 years before
finally switching over to Werbel &amp; McMillen.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:53.790
He didn’t stick around for long here either
as he left just 1 year later in 1984, but

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:56.830
this was his last job as a traditional lawyer.

00:02:56.830 --> 00:03:00.920
Ever since then, Papa Powell has been all
about the money.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:06.609
MONEY POWELL:
In 1984, Jerome picked up a job at an investment

00:03:06.609 --> 00:03:08.319
bank called Dillon, Read, &amp; Co.

00:03:08.319 --> 00:03:13.400
Initially, he focused on the legal side of
financing, merchant banking, and mergers/acquisitions.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:18.480
But, with time, he was able to climb the corporate
ladder and become vice president.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:23.250
He worked here for a total of 6 years before
he jumped over to the US Treasury in 1990.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:25.599
Jerome wasn’t the first one to make this
jump actually.

00:03:25.599 --> 00:03:30.580
In 1988, the chairman of Dillon, Read, &amp; Co,
Nicholas F. Brady, left his position after

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:34.250
being appointed as the US Secretary of the
Treasury by President Ronald Reagan.

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:39.489
I’m not sure if Nicholas put in a good word
for Jerome but in 1992, Jerome was appointed

00:03:39.489 --> 00:03:43.730
as the Under Secretary of the Treasury for
Domestic Finance by President George H.W.

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:44.730
Bush.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:48.779
This was perfect timing for Jerome as this
put him in charge of overseeing a treasury

00:03:48.779 --> 00:03:49.809
bond scandal.

00:03:49.809 --> 00:03:54.049
If you weren’t aware, there’s a limit
to buying treasury bonds during initial offerings.

00:03:54.049 --> 00:03:58.650
Any individual person or entity can only buy
a maximum of $5 million worth of treasury

00:03:58.650 --> 00:03:59.739
bonds during each offering.

00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:04.519
A trader at Salomon Brothers named Paul Mozer,
however, was submitting false bids in an effort

00:04:04.519 --> 00:04:08.340
to bypass the bond limit, but he of course
got caught.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:12.669
And Jerome was in charge of overseeing the
investigation and sanctioning Salomon Brothers.

00:04:12.669 --> 00:04:17.900
Salomon ended up being fined $190 million
and they were forced to set aside $100 million

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:19.530
as a restitution fund.

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:23.900
The CEO John Gutfreund was also barred from
serving as the CEO of any other brokerage

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:24.900
firm.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:28.120
Just as this case wrapped up though, so did
Jerome’s time at the treasury.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:33.080
In 1993, President Bill Clinton took office,
and he brought with him a new slew of secretaries.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:37.020
So, Jerome returned to the private industry
and picked up a job as a managing director

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:38.720
at Bankers Trust.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:42.950
But unfortunately, the trust would get stuck
up in some legal trouble right after Jerome

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:43.950
joined.

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:48.070
You see, Bankers Trust had convinced some
big corporate clients including P&amp;G that they

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:51.620
should invest some of their money in riskier
investments such as derivatives.

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:56.040
These clients agreed and Bankers Trust went
ahead and made these investments, but as you

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:58.790
might’ve guessed, this led to substantial
losses.

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:02.750
And the clients ended up suing Bankers Trust
for misrepresenting the risk involved and

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:03.970
they would win.

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:09.070
Clearly, this was not somewhere that Jerome
wanted to be around for long, so in 1995,

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:12.260
he packed his bags and returned to Dillon,
Read, &amp; Co.

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:16.400
But this, Jerome also had a bunch of side
hustles going on as well.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:21.090
For example, in 1997, he became a partner
at the Carlyle Group and founded the Industrial

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:22.090
Group.

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:25.890
He worked there till 2005 at which point he
cofounded a private investment firm called

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:27.400
Severn Capital Partners.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:31.720
The firm was focused on leveraging opportunistic
investments within the industrial sector.

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:36.060
And finally, in 2008, Jerome became a managing
partner at the Global Environment Fund.

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:40.450
As the name suggests, the firm was focused
on making sustainable energy investments.

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:45.680
While all these side hustles and jobs kept
Jerome busy for nearly 20 years, he did eventually

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:48.540
circle back to politics.

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:53.580
HAWK POWELL:
Starting in 2010, Jerome served as a visiting

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:58.850
scholar at the Bipartisan Policy Center, and
this was once again perfect timing for Jerome

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:02.290
as the debt-ceiling crisis rolled around in
2011.

00:06:02.290 --> 00:06:05.940
If you follow politics, you’ll know that
every few years, Congress has to raise the

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:07.000
debt ceiling.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:10.860
If they don’t, the government will run out
of money and default on their loans.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:15.450
Usually, it’s a given that Congress will
raise the debt ceiling, but politicians often

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:17.600
like to use it as a bargaining chip.

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:20.920
For example, the Democrats might say that
they’ll help the Republicans raise the debt

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:24.900
ceiling as soon as the Republicans help them
approve another round of stimulus checks or

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:26.460
raise the minimum wage.

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:30.740
Generally, one side gives in close to the
deadline and things work out.

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:35.830
But every so often, neither side gives in
and we enter the risk of default, and one

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:38.690
of these situations unfolded in 2011.

00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:42.670
As a visiting scholar, Jerome became one of
the leading voices advocating for bipartisan

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:44.620
support in raising the debt ceiling.

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:48.770
He presented all of the negative implications
of not raising the debt ceiling such as ruined

00:06:48.770 --> 00:06:50.960
credit and an irreparable reputation.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:55.810
It’s not like the other politicians weren’t
aware of these implications, but the Bipartisan

00:06:55.810 --> 00:06:58.390
Policy Center ramped up the pressure to the
max.

00:06:58.390 --> 00:07:02.590
Congress did eventually raise the debt ceiling,
but it wasn’t before being downgraded from

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:05.270
AAA to AA+ by the S&amp;P.

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:09.140
In the meantime, President Obama was having
a hard time filling a seat at the Federal

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:10.160
Reserve Board of Governors.

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:15.140
He had initially nominated a democrat named
Jeremy C. Stein, but Senate Republicans refused

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:16.470
to approve his nomination.

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:21.220
Obama had to nominate someone that Republicans
would accept, and who better to nominate than

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:24.330
the bipartisan man who was advocating for
the debt ceiling to be raised.

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:28.660
And with that, Obama nominated Jerome Powell
to the Federal Reserve Board of Governors

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:31.669
and he took office on May 25, 2012.

00:07:31.669 --> 00:07:36.430
Two years later, Obama renominated him for
a 14-year term, and the Senate approved this.

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:39.450
So, Jerome isn’t going anywhere till at
least 2028.

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:43.790
Now, this doesn’t mean that he’ll remain
chairman, but he will at least be a board

00:07:43.790 --> 00:07:44.790
member.

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:48.080
Anyway, ever since Jerome took office, he’s
actually mostly been a hawk.

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:52.200
In other words, he’s mostly supported tightening
of monetary policy.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:56.980
In September of 2012, for instance, he was
a massive skeptic of a third round of quantitative

00:07:56.980 --> 00:07:57.980
easing.

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:01.530
Similarly, one of his biggest goals has been
ending banks that are quote-on-quote too big

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:02.530
to fail.

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:07.220
In 2017, he described the current state of
Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac, the two biggest

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:10.600
mortgage providers is quote “unacceptable
and unsustainable”.

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:14.950
He further elaborated that the next couple
of years may be our last chance to end their

00:08:14.950 --> 00:08:18.350
monopoly and prevent past mistakes from being
repeated.

00:08:18.350 --> 00:08:22.570
And Jerome soon got the perfect opportunity
to take action as President Trump nominated

00:08:22.570 --> 00:08:26.340
him for chair of the Federal Reserve in November
of 2017.

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:31.560
PAPA POWELL:
As soon as Jerome became chairman, he continued

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:35.310
to raise interest rates and announced his
plans to reduce the Fed’s balance sheet.

00:08:35.310 --> 00:08:39.930
He wanted to reduce their holdings from $4.5
trillion to $2.5 to $3 trillion.

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:44.520
At first, investors didn’t care too much,
but as the effects of his tightening started

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:47.670
to take hold, they became more and more irritated.

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:53.470
In the last quarter of 2018, indices like
the S&amp;P 500 and Nasdaq corrected a full 20%,

00:08:53.470 --> 00:08:56.660
and Trump was not happy by any means.

00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:00.790
One of Trump’s signature bragging points
was how good the stock market was doing.

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:05.150
But with Jerome tightening, the stock market
was no longer doing that well.

00:09:05.150 --> 00:09:09.860
So, Trump started to publicly criticize Jerome
for doing a terrible job and started discussing

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:12.250
the possibility of firing Jerome.

00:09:12.250 --> 00:09:16.330
With all this mounting pressure, Jerome backed
off on the quantitative tightening, but he

00:09:16.330 --> 00:09:20.000
made it clear that he was not going to return
to quantitative easing.

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:24.010
He was just giving the market a chance to
breathe and he would return to quantitative

00:09:24.010 --> 00:09:25.570
tightening when appropriate.

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:29.610
You could say this aged like milk though as
Covid came around just a couple of months

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:34.230
later, and Papa Powell turned on the money
printer and it hasn’t stopped since.

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:38.190
This has led to exponential runs in growth
stocks, cryptocurrencies, real estate, and

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:40.050
basically everything you can think of.

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:43.540
From the very beginning, a lot of critics
argued that this excessive money printing

00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:45.330
would lead to excessive inflation.

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:48.060
Jerome acknowledged these concerns from the
very beginning by arguing that this inflation

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:52.020
would be transitory, but this is honestly
super misleading.

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:55.800
When people hear that inflation will be transitory,
they’re led to believe that price hikes

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:58.640
are temporary and that prices will go down.

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:01.860
But this is not what transitory inflation
means.

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:05.040
Transitory inflation simply means that the
inflation rate will go back down to average

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:06.040
numbers.

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:09.310
For example, let’s say an iPhone costs $1000.

00:10:09.310 --> 00:10:14.810
If we see super bad inflation of 10% one year,
the price of the iPhone will rise to $1100.

00:10:14.810 --> 00:10:19.400
Even if inflation drops 2% the next year and
the inflation was “transitory”, the price

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:21.870
of the iPhone would still rise to $1122.

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:28.020
For prices to drop, we would need 9% deflation
and Jerome has by no means promised that.

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:32.380
So, the truth is, a lot of the price hikes
we’ve seen are here to stay.

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:36.350
But recently, Jerome has changed his tone
when it comes to inflation probably because

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:37.720
of Joe Biden.

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:42.830
All this rampant inflation is making Biden
look bad, and mid-term elections are coming

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:43.830
up.

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:48.520
At the same time, Jerome needed Biden to renominate
him, so it’s likely that Jerome has become

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:50.450
more hawkish to please Biden.

00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:53.500
In the most recent Federal Reserve meeting,
Jerome announced that they will complete their

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:58.060
taper by early march, and that rate hikes
and quantitative tightening are coming soon

00:10:58.060 --> 00:10:59.060
after.

00:10:59.060 --> 00:11:03.280
So, it looks like the glorious or not glorious
days of the money printer are finally coming

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:04.480
to an end.

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:08.390
But there’s no doubt that Jerome will forever
be known as the legendary money printer of

00:11:08.390 --> 00:11:09.480
the Covid pandemic.

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:12.270
Did Jerome’s money printer help you or hurt
you?

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:13.610
Comment that down below.

00:11:13.610 --> 00:11:17.529
Also, drop a like if you wish you could print
money just like Papa Powell.

00:11:17.529 --> 00:11:21.600
